        "cross_source_automatic_exact_missing_authorship|admitted by the missing-authorship fallback, which fires precisely when authorship is absent on one or both sides. Weaker still than the strict rule, and rejected for the same reason plus an unverified homonym guard.": 2133,
        "cross_source_automatic_exact_strict|the conservative exact rule matches on canonical name and rank and then strengthens the match with authorship, status and kingdom agreement plus a homonym guard. That is a stronger name match, but it is still a name match: neither source published a cross-reference asserting the two concepts are the same. continuity_rules.canonical_name_only requires review for exactly this evidence, so the binding enriches names and must not be published as concept identity. Reviewable candidates are identified by scripts/cross_reference_evidence.py, which reports the sources' own synonymy assertions; a human decision on that evidence is what promotes an association, one at a time.": 14291,
        "intra_source_synonym|a synonym usage bound to its own accepted concept inside one source. Its relation to the backbone concept is transitive on the accepted usage's binding and has not been graded on its own evidence; admitting it would emit an identifier whose evidence was never evaluated.": 30225
